# Registered holes in the engine/runtime split, checked by tools/check-layering.sh.
#
# src/engine/ is meant to be the ECMAScript language and nothing else, and
# src/runtime/ the host bindings layered on top. Where that is not yet true, the
# exception is argued HERE rather than in a comment at the top of one source file
# where nobody reads it — the same reason tests/.node-oracle-exempt exists.
#
# The gate is a ratchet in both directions: an unregistered violation fails, and
# so does a registered exemption that no longer applies. The list only shrinks.
#
# Three record shapes:
#
#   <engine file> -> <runtime file>    an import edge from the engine side
#   <engine file> -> std/<module>      an engine-side import of a HOST std module
#                                      (fs, os, process, net, fetch, sqlite, env, args,
#                                      argparse); no project file is crossed, so the
#                                      first shape never shows it
#   global: <name> <class> <reason>    a `__`-prefixed global src/engine/bootstrap.milo
#                                      installs, so the milojs-engine binary exposes it.
#                                      class is engine-intrinsic or host-native.


# --- IMPORT EDGES ---
#
# All four are pre-existing and structural, not accidents of the file move. They
# are the debt the split made visible.

src/engine/eval.milo -> src/runtime/host.milo    eval.milo owns the single Builtin dispatch switch, and the host natives (sqlite, fetch, spawn) are arms of it. host.milo's own header states the intended relationship: "milojs-engine links this file and never calls into it; only the milojs runtime does." The link is real, the call never happens in the engine binary. Removing the edge means lifting ~60 arms out of a 16.6k-line switch behind an indirection, which is a refactor, not a move.
src/engine/eval.milo -> src/runtime/napi.milo    same shape: the Node-API natives are dispatched from that same switch. Node-API is host by definition, so this edge is pure debt with no language argument behind it.
src/engine/eval.milo -> src/runtime/modules.milo    the evaluator has to turn a specifier into a module record to execute `import`, `export` and dynamic `import()`, which ARE the language. But modules.milo is two things in one file: that graph loader, and node's resolution algorithm (node_modules walk, package.json main, ~50 embedded node builtin shims). Only the first half belongs to the engine. Splitting the file is the fix; the edge stands until it happens.
src/milojs-engine.milo -> src/runtime/modules.milo    the engine entry point calls preloadGraph to load its OWN built-ins — lib/engine-prelude.js (spec built-ins written in JS) and lib/temporal.js — before any user code runs. So the engine genuinely needs a module loader; it does not need node's resolver, and today those are the same file. Not removable without the split above.


# --- HOST STD EDGES ---
#
# One line per (file, module). The engine binary links every one of these; what
# keeps it honest is that half 3 of the gate runs it and finds no host native
# reachable from JS. The edges are the same debt as the import edges above: the
# evaluator's Builtin switch still holds the host arms (ReadFileSync, TcpListen,
# SpawnSync, SqliteOpen and ~60 more), and Interp still carries the host handle
# tables. Lifting those out is the modules.milo/host.milo split, not a move.
# std/os is listed where only libc plumbing is used, because the ratchet keys on
# the module, and the argument says so.

src/engine/eval.milo -> std/fs    the Builtin switch's fs arms: ReadFileSync reads through readFile, and WriteFileSync, ReadDir, Mkdir, Stat, Realpath and the rest are the second std/fs import block at the same site. Host arms in the evaluator, see host.milo.
src/engine/eval.milo -> std/os    the same arms' fd-level half (open, read, write, lseek, fsync, ftruncate, fchmod, close), the raw socket for Builtin.UdpBind, kill for child processes, and exit for process.exit. Capability, not plumbing.
src/engine/eval.milo -> std/process    Builtin.SpawnSync and the async spawn arms drive Child.spawn and read the pipes; exePath answers process.execPath.
src/engine/eval.milo -> std/env    a member read on the process.env object (isEnv) falls through to Env.get for names the object does not hold yet.
src/engine/eval.milo -> std/net    Builtin.TcpListen/TcpConnect arms construct TcpListener/TcpStream; the handles live in Interp.listeners/conns (see runtime.milo below).
src/engine/eval.milo -> std/sqlite    the Builtin.Sqlite* arms (open, close, exec, query, step, bind, column) are the node:sqlite binding, dispatched from the evaluator.
src/engine/runtime.milo -> std/net    Interp owns the open-socket tables (listeners: Vec<TcpListener>, conns: Vec<TcpStream>) because the evaluator's arms above need somewhere to keep them across calls; type-only, no I/O happens here.
src/engine/runtime.milo -> std/sqlite    same: Interp.sqliteDbs/sqliteStmts hold Database/Statement handles for the sqlite arms. Type-only.
src/engine/runtime.milo -> std/os    free only: releasing a typed array's malloc'd backing buffer in the collector. Plumbing, not a capability.
src/engine/value.milo -> std/os    snprintf only: number-to-string formatting for JS Number stringification. Plumbing, not a capability.
src/milojs-engine.milo -> std/fs    the engine binary's own entry point reads the script file it was handed. An entry point, not the language; it stays on the engine side of the gate because it must link nothing from src/runtime/.
src/milojs-engine.milo -> std/env    MILOJS_* environment switches for the engine binary's CLI.
src/milojs-engine.milo -> std/os    exit with the script's status code.
src/milojs-engine.milo -> std/argparse    the engine binary's command line.
src/milojs-engine.milo -> std/args    the engine binary's command line.


# --- ENGINE GLOBAL SURFACE ---
#
# `milojs-engine` answers `function` for __sqliteOpen. It also does for
# __httpFetch, __spawnSync, __tcpListen, __readFileSync and 66 others: the whole
# host native surface is installed by src/engine/bootstrap.milo, which the engine
# binary runs. `fetch` is undefined there only because `fetch` is the JS wrapper
# in lib/node-fetch.js, which the engine never loads — the native underneath it
# is present. sqlite is not the odd one out; it is one of 71.
#
# They are registered rather than removed because gating them out is the same
# refactor as the eval->host edge above: the natives are arms of the evaluator's
# dispatch switch, and bootstrap installs a name for every arm. A conditional
# install would be a second, weaker place for the layering to be true, and would
# not remove the code from the engine binary anyway.
#
# What this ledger buys: adding a new host native to the engine's bootstrap now
# costs a line and an argument here instead of nothing.

global: __byteLength                 engine-intrinsic ArrayBuffer/TypedArray byte length; the spec's [[ArrayBufferByteLength]] read, no host in it
global: __callFrames                 engine-intrinsic the interpreter's own call stack, which is what Error.prototype.stack is built from
global: __construct                  engine-intrinsic [[Construct]] on a callable; Reflect.construct and the prelude's class helpers call it
global: __inspect                    engine-intrinsic the value formatter behind console.log, which is part of the engine's built-in global set
global: __realmCreate                engine-intrinsic a new realm (its own intrinsics and global scope); test262's $262.createRealm is built on it, and realms are the language's, not the host's
global: __realmEval                  engine-intrinsic ScriptEvaluation in the realm a global object belongs to; test262's $262.evalScript, no host in it
global: __isConstructor              engine-intrinsic IsConstructor(); the prelude needs the spec predicate, not a heuristic
global: __iteratorProto              engine-intrinsic %IteratorPrototype%, an intrinsic the prelude cannot reach any other way
global: __markNotConstructor         engine-intrinsic marks a prelude-defined function non-constructable, as the spec requires of methods
global: __promiseResolveValue        engine-intrinsic the Promise resolve/reject machinery the prelude's Promise combinators drive
global: __reflectDefineProperty      engine-intrinsic Reflect.defineProperty; the prelude implements the Reflect namespace in JS over these
global: __reflectGet                 engine-intrinsic Reflect.get, same reason
global: __reflectDelete              engine-intrinsic Reflect.deleteProperty; it cannot be `delete target[key]` in the prelude because that file is strict and strict delete throws where Reflect must answer false
global: __reflectOwnKeys             engine-intrinsic Reflect.ownKeys, same reason
global: __reflectPreventExtensions   engine-intrinsic Reflect.preventExtensions, same reason
global: __reflectSet                 engine-intrinsic Reflect.set, same reason
global: __reflectSetPrototypeOf      engine-intrinsic Reflect.setPrototypeOf, same reason
global: __timerRef                  engine-intrinsic the ref flag on a timer in the engine's own queue, which setTimeout/setInterval put there; the prelude's Timeout.ref/unref have no other way to reach it
global: __jsonStringifyFast         engine-intrinsic the native JSON.stringify fast path over plain data; the prelude wrapper calls it first and falls back to its own walker when it answers undefined
